SimpleDock 1.1 (16.10.2026)
A simple, macOS-inspired dock for AmigaOS 3.x

Copyright (C) 2026 renewable-intel
Free software under the GNU General Public License, version 3 or later
(see LICENSE). It comes with ABSOLUTELY NO WARRANTY.

Source code: https://github.com/renewable-intel/SimpleDock
This version: https://github.com/renewable-intel/SimpleDock/releases/tag/v1.1


WHAT IT IS
----------

A macOS-like dock for your Workbench.

Launch programs with a click, drag icons to rearrange them, and add icons
from Workbench. The dock shows which programs are running, also those
that are not on it. It brings them to the front and lists their windows.
Files dropped onto a drawer icon are copied into it. Choose a solid or
tinted transparent background, display labels below icons or on hover,
and use auto-hide to keep the dock out of the way. A configurable hotkey
brings it back into view.


REQUIREMENTS
------------

Minimum
  - AmigaOS 3.2 (best), or 3.1.4, 3.5 or 3.9 (also 3.1-based systems
    that carry those libraries): workbench.library and icon.library
    44 or newer are needed
  - A 68030 with Fast RAM
    (it also runs on a stock 68020 A1200, but animations are slow)

Recommended
  - A 68040 or 68060 accelerator, or an accelerated Amiga such as a
    PiStorm / Emu68 system
  - A graphics card (Picasso96) with a 16- or 24-bit screen mode,
    for smooth animations and a fast transparent dock

SimpleDock also works on AGA (8-bit) screens, more slowly. Picasso96 is
used when present but is not required.

The drop-down lists of the settings window use ReAction's
chooser.gadget, part of OS 3.2. Without it, they are GadTools cycle
gadgets, which step to the next choice with each click.


INSTALLING
----------

Place the SimpleDock drawer wherever you prefer, then double-click
SimpleDock to run it. No installer is required.

The drawer has no icon of its own, so Workbench uses your default drawer
icon. If it is not visible, choose Window > Show > All Files, then use
Icons > Snapshot to give it an icon.

To start SimpleDock with Workbench, put a copy of the SimpleDock icon
(or the program) in SYS:WBStartup.

On first launch, SimpleDock creates ENVARC:SimpleDock.config and adds
standard Workbench icons found on your system: the Workbench disk, Shell,
MultiView, Clock, Calculator, IconEdit, Exchange, the Prefs, Tools and
Utilities drawers, and Ram Disk. It adds as many as fit on your screen.

To remove SimpleDock, delete its drawer and ENVARC:SimpleDock.config.
Also remove any copy you placed in SYS:WBStartup.


USING IT
--------

Click            Launches a program or opens a drawer, disk or project.
                 If the program is already running, all its windows
                 (every copy) come to the front instead; click again
                 to go through them one by one. Minimised windows
                 stay minimised - unless all of them are, then the
                 click brings them back.

Shift+click      Starts another copy of the program. (With Click when
                 running set to New copy, the two swap: a click starts
                 a copy, Shift+click brings the windows to the front.)

Del              With the pointer over a running program's icon:
                 closes the program (all its windows, as Close All).
                 Anywhere else Del goes to your programs as usual.

Long press       Hold the button on a running program's icon for a
                 moment: a list of its windows opens, oldest first.
                 Let go over a window to bring it to the front, or
                 over an action to do that. Let go anywhere else and
                 the list stays open:
                   - click a window in the list to bring it forward
                   - point at one and press Del to close it, as its
                     close gadget would (the program may ask first;
                     a Shell gets Ctrl+\, which ends it if no command
                     is running)
                   - below a line, what can be done with the
                     program (click a row, or point at it and press
                     Del):
                       Keep in Dock / Remove from Dock
                       Show in Workbench (opens its drawer)
                       Minimise / Show (n) (hides its windows; Show
                         gives the number minimised and brings them
                         back; with some minimised and some not, both
                         are there; a minimised program's icon cannot
                         be dragged)
                       Close
                       Force Quit... (only once Close was tried and
                         the program is still there): first the break
                         signals Ctrl+C, D, E, F, which stop Shell
                         programs and ports; still there after two
                         seconds, it asks, then ends the program and
                         closes its windows. Unsaved work in it is
                         lost; what it held (memory, files, locks)
                         stays in use until a reboot, and a program
                         ended inside a system call can take the
                         system down - the same as Scout's Remove.
                         Save your work first.
                 With several windows these read Minimise...,
                 Show... and Close...: the list then shows those
                 windows, with Minimise All / Show All / Close All at
                 the bottom. Pick one window at a time; the list stays
                 open for more until none is left (Esc or a click
                 elsewhere closes it; a click on the dock then only
                 closes the list, it doesn't click an icon).
                 A program on the dock that is not running also gets
                 the list, with Remove from Dock and Show in Workbench.
                   - Esc or a click elsewhere closes the list
                 To drag the icon instead, keep the button down and
                 slide along the dock: the list closes and the drag
                 starts. Let go off the dock and the icon goes back.

Drag along       Repositions an icon; the other icons move aside.

Drag off         Removes the icon from the dock (not from the disk).

Drop icons       Drag icons from Workbench onto the dock:
                   - between two icons: they are added there
                   - onto a program icon: they are opened with it (for
                     example a text file onto your editor)
                   - onto a drawer or disk icon: they are copied into
                     it with their icons, keeping dates, protection
                     bits and comments (at most 32 icons at a time).
                     With "Drop on a drawer" set to Like Workbench,
                     they are moved instead when on the same disk.
                     If a file of that name is already there, you are
                     asked: Replace or Skip. Drawers are never merged:
                     a drawer of that name is left alone.
                 A beep means a drop was refused, in part or in full:
                 a drawer into itself or one of its own drawers, an
                 icon into the drawer it is already in, a whole disk,
                 a drawer and a file of the same name, or a drop onto
                 the Trash.

Missing item     An icon is dimmed and marked with "?" if its program,
                 file, drawer or disk is unavailable. Click it to choose
                 whether to remove it from the dock. SimpleDock never
                 removes it automatically: an item on a removable disk
                 becomes available again when the disk is inserted.

Full dock        The dock is always one row. It holds as many icons as
                 fit along the screen edge at the icon size and spacing
                 you choose. When full, the dock appears greyed out if
                 you drag icons over a gap. Dropping there sounds a beep
                 without adding the icons. You can still drop files onto
                 an icon to open them with that program. Settings that
                 would leave icons off the dock are rejected with a beep.

Hotkey           CTRL ALT SHIFT: press the three together and let go
                 (change it in the settings window). Programs that use
                 Ctrl+Alt+Shift with another key are not affected. With
                 auto-hide it is a toggle: hidden (or covered), the dock
                 comes out in front and stays until the pointer has
                 entered and left it; out, it hides and stays hidden
                 until the pointer has left the edge. Press it before
                 dragging an icon onto a hidden dock. Without auto-hide
                 it brings the dock to the front.

Ctrl+Alt+M       Minimises the active window (as Minimise in the
                 long-press list).
Ctrl+Alt+Shift+M Minimises all, by what is active: a drawer window - all
                 drawer windows; a program's window - all its windows
                 (every copy; for a Shell, all Shells); nothing, or the
                 Workbench backdrop - every window on the screen. With
                 nothing showing any more, the same key brings back all
                 the windows the dock has minimised.
Ctrl+Alt+H       Switches auto-hide on or off (kept in the settings).
                 These three keys can be changed or switched off (NONE)
                 in the settings window: Advanced, Keys.

Dock menu        In the Workbench menu bar (shown when Workbench is the
                 active window):
                   Show / Hide Dock   as the hotkey
                   Settings...        the settings window
                   About...
                   Quit

Changes made with the mouse are saved automatically. Adding, moving or
removing an icon saves immediately; settings are saved when you close
the settings window.


RUNNING PROGRAMS
----------------

A dot under an icon shows that its program is running. With two or more
copies, their number appears on the icon, up to 99. (With
Running programs set to Dot, the hover label shows it instead: "Clock
(2 running)".)

A click on a running program brings all its windows to the front, of
every copy, and makes the frontmost one active. If they are all in
front already, each further click brings the next one forward, so you
can go through them. Shift+click starts a new copy.

SimpleDock starts programs itself, the way Workbench does, so it knows
at once when a program it started ends. A program started elsewhere,
from Workbench or a Shell, is noticed when one of its windows becomes
active, or within 2 seconds.

The Shell icon counts your open Shells and works like a program icon: a
click brings the Shells to the front, Shift+click opens a new one, a
long press lists their windows, with Minimise / Show and Close (each
Shell gets Ctrl+\, which ends it if no command is running).

Programs that are not on the dock show up too, at its end behind a thin
divider, with their own icon (or the standard one if their file is not
found). They work like the icons on the dock: click, Shift+click, long
press, drop files onto them. When the program ends, its icon goes. Open
Shells show there as one Shell icon if the Shell is not on the dock.

While Workbench has drawer windows open, a Workbench icon (the standard
drawer icon) sits right after the divider, in front of the other
programs (a second divider separates them), with the number of windows
from two on. A click brings all the drawer windows to the front; a long
press lists them, with Minimise / Show and Close, as for a program. It
cannot be kept or dragged; files dropped onto it are added at the end
of your dock icons. Close All closes the drawer windows one right after
another. To do without it, set Workbench icon to Hide (Advanced,
Programs).

A drawer or disk on the dock that is open in Workbench gets a dot too.
A click brings its window to the front (or opens it if it isn't open);
a long press lists its windows with Minimise / Show and Close. Its
windows are also in the Workbench icon's list, and a change made from
either shows on both. This needs workbench.library 44 (3.1.4 and
later); windows are told by their title, so two open drawers of the
same name (on different disks) both count for each.

To keep one of them, drag it in front of the divider (also after a long
press: slide it along the dock). If SimpleDock does not know where the
program's file is, a file requester asks. A dock icon you drag off while
its program runs moves behind the divider until the program ends.

Small tools that only show a borderless strip, such as a clock in the
screen title bar, are left out. To hide any other program, drag its icon
off the dock: SimpleDock asks first, then does not show it again. The
hidden programs are listed in the settings window (Advanced, Hidden
programs, IGNORE); remove a name there to show it again.

When the dock is full, its last place holds the rest: an icon with three
dots and their number. Click it for a list of those programs; pick one
to bring it to the front. If your own icons fill the whole dock, the
last of them makes room for it while those programs run.

With auto-hide, the dock is updated while it is hidden, so it comes out
ready.

All of this can be changed or switched off under Programs on the
Advanced side of the settings window.


SETTINGS WINDOW
---------------

Click a list to see its choices, then select one. The settings are in
groups:

Place
  Position         Top, Bottom, Left or Right edge
  Gap to edge      space between the dock and the screen edge
  Auto-hide        off, or how fast the dock slides away
  In front         keep the dock in front of other windows
  Hotkey           the keys that bring the dock out. CTRL ALT SHIFT is
                   the default (modifiers only: pressed together, then
                   let go); you can type another combination, for
                   example CTRL ALT D or CTRL HELP (Ctrl + Help)
Icons
  Icon size        original size, or shrunk to small / medium / large
  Icon spacing     space between the icons
  Text             labels below the icons, on hover, off, or Labels
                   only (the names without the icons)
Look
  Colour           six colours or your own. The "Custom..." button next
                   to it opens a colour picker (red, green and blue
                   sliders); OK adds that colour to the list (as
                   #RRGGBB) and uses it. There is one custom colour;
                   picking a new one replaces it.
  Transparent      Off: a solid dock in the colour. On: the screen shows
                   through the dock, tinted with the colour.
  Opacity          how dark the Transparent dock is (greyed out while
                   Transparent is Off)
  Corners          square to round

Click Advanced >> to show the advanced settings beside these. On smaller
screens they take their place. Click << Simple to hide them again. The
settings file keyword of each is given in parentheses here.

Fine-tune
  Alignment        Centre, Start or End along the edge (POS)
  Offset           shift along the edge, in pixels (OFFSET)
  Gap to edge      any number of pixels (DISTANCE)
  Icon spacing     any number of pixels (ICONDIST)
  Icon size        shrink icons bigger than this many pixels, 0 = own
                   size (SCALEICONW and SCALEICONH together)
  Most icons       maximum number of icons, 0 = as many as fit (LIMIT)
  Corner radius    in pixels, 0-32 (RADIUS)
  Opacity          0-100, for the Transparent look (OPACITY)
  Auto-hide speed  0 = off; a slide takes this x 70 ms (AUTOHIDE)
  Colour           any colour as #rrggbb (TINT)
Programs
  Running programs Off (no dots, no checking), Dot, or Dot and number
                   (the default) (RUNNING=0/1/2)
  Click when       Bring to front, or New copy (the classic Amiga way;
  running          Shift+click then brings it to the front) (CLICKNEW)
  Start programs   SimpleDock (it then knows at once when a program
  with             ends), or Workbench (as in 1.0, for a program that
                   does not like being started by the dock) (WBSTART).
                   Icons set to start from a Shell (CLI tooltype), ARexx
                   icons (REXX) and programs without an icon of their own
                   are always started by Workbench, as a double-click.
  Shell icons      Start at once, or Ask first: those icons show an
                   "Execute a file" requester with their command, as a
                   double-click does (not for icons with DONOTPROMPT)
                   (SHELLASK)
  Drop on a drawer Copy, or Like Workbench: moved when on the same disk,
                   copied from another disk (DROPMOVE)
  Long press       Normal, Long or Longer: 0.3 / 0.4 / 0.5 s
                   (HOLDTIME=3/4/5, tenths of a second)
  Programs not on  Show or Hide the programs that are not on the dock
  the dock         (RUNHIDE)
  Workbench icon   Show or Hide the Workbench icon (WBHIDE)
  Hidden programs  the programs you dragged off the dock. Pick one and
                   click Remove to show it again (IGNORE)
Shell drops
  Icons into a     Type paths (default) or Off: icons dropped from
  Shell            Workbench onto a Shell window have their paths typed
                   at the cursor (in quotes if needed). A single archive
                   whose tool is in C: or Workbench's path gets the
                   command that unpacks it instead, e.g. lha x "Work:a.lha"
                   - check it and press Return. Archives: .lha .lzh .lzx
                   .zip .tar .tgz .tar.gz .gz .bz2 .xz .7z .rar (lha, lzx,
                   unzip, tar, gzip, bzip2, xz, 7z, unrar). In the standard
                   console and in others such as KingCON; not on 3.2,
                   whose Shell types paths itself. A console that types
                   them itself too would get them twice: then Off
                   (SHELLDROP)
                   Icons dropped onto the dock's Shell icon go into the
                   Shell window in front the same way (on any system),
                   and that Shell is brought to the front; with no Shell
                   open, a new one starts and gets them.
  Unpack archives  into the Shell's current drawer, RAM:, or the Unpack
  to               drawer (UNPACKTO=0/1/2)
  Unpack drawer    the drawer for that (UNPACKDIR)
Keys
  Minimise window  the key that minimises the active window (MINKEY)
  Minimise all     the key that minimises all (MINALLKEY)
  Auto-hide on/off the key that switches auto-hide (AHKEY)
                   Type a key such as CTRL ALT M; NONE or empty switches
                   it off. Modifiers alone (CTRL ALT) are only allowed
                   for the Hotkey.
System
  Font             name/size for the labels, empty = screen font (FONT)
  Screen           a public screen, empty = Workbench (PUBSCREEN)
  Start delay      wait n/50 s before the dock opens (DELAY)
  Messages         Normal; Quiet (no message requesters at all); Verbose
                   (also messages that are normally silent, for example
                   a hotkey that is not valid) (QUIET, VERBOSE)
  Crash reports    off by default; see BUG REPORTS below (CRASHLOG)
  No border        no thin border around the dock (NOBORDER)

Numeric and text values take effect when you press Return or Tab.
Double-click a text field (Colour, Font, Screen) to mark all of it:
typing or Del then replaces it in one go. This needs the string.gadget
of OS 3.2; Right-Amiga+X clears a field in any case. The two sides stay
in sync: choosing Large corners sets the corner radius to 18. A value
without a matching preset is shown as "Other" on the simple side.

Load... reads another settings file (its settings and icons) into the
dock; they are kept when you close the window. Save as... writes a copy
of your settings and icons to another file. SimpleDock always saves to
and starts from its own file, ENVARC:SimpleDock.config.

Changes appear immediately and are saved when you close the window.
With auto-hide enabled, the dock stays visible while the settings window
is open and hides again when you close it.

Undo changes restores the settings in use when the window opened, on
both sides. It remains greyed out until you make a change. Invalid
values and changes that would leave icons off the dock are rejected
with a beep; the previous value is restored.


SETTINGS FILE AND TOOLTYPES
---------------------------

ENVARC:SimpleDock.config is plain text: settings lines first, then one
line per icon (the path of the icon, without .info). A keyword in
parentheses is disabled. Example:

  ; SimpleDock settings - a keyword in parentheses is disabled
  POS=B
  HOVERTEXT
  (AUTOHIDE=3)
  TINT=#383838
  ; icons
  SYS:Utilities/MultiView
  LABEL Viewer
  SYS:System/Shell

LABEL changes the label of the preceding icon. IMAGE <path> uses the
image from another icon.

The same keywords work as tooltypes of the SimpleDock icon and on the
command line:

  CONFIG=<file>       settings file (default ENVARC:SimpleDock.config)
  POS=<T|B|L|R>       screen edge, with an optional alignment letter
  DISTANCE=<n>        gap to the screen edge in pixels (default 4)
  OFFSET=<n>          shift along the edge in pixels
  FONT=<name/size>    font for the labels
  PUBSCREEN=<name>    public screen to open on
  NOTEXT              no labels
  HOVERTEXT           a name only for the icon under the pointer
  NOICON              the names without the icons (Text: Labels only)
  NOBORDER            no thin border
  ICONDIST=<n>        extra space between icons (default 2)
  SCALEICONW=<n>      shrink icons wider than this (0 = own size)
  SCALEICONH=<n>      shrink icons taller than this
  LIMIT=<n>           at most n icons (default: as many as fit)
  AUTOHIDE=<n>        auto-hide; a slide takes n x 70 ms
  ONTOP               stay in front of other windows
  TRANSPARENT         transparent (tinted) background
  TINT=#rrggbb        colour (default #383838)
  OPACITY=<n>         darkness of the transparent background, 0-100
  RADIUS=<n>          corner radius in pixels (default 12)
  DELAY=<n>           wait n/50 s before opening
  HOTKEY=<keys>       hotkey (default ctrl alt shift)
  QUIET / VERBOSE     fewer / more messages
  CUSTOMTINT=#rrggbb  the custom colour of the settings window's list
  CRASHLOG            crash reports (see BUG REPORTS)
  RUNNING=<n>         0 = no running dots, 1 = dot, 2 = dot and number
                      (default 2)
  CLICKNEW            a click starts a new copy (Shift+click: to the front)
  WBSTART             Workbench starts the programs, not the dock
  DROPMOVE            drops onto a drawer move on the same disk
  HOLDTIME=<n>        long press, in tenths of a second (default 3)
  RUNHIDE             programs not on the dock are not shown
  IGNORE=<names>      ... nor these programs ("Name,Name")
  WBHIDE              no Workbench icon for the drawer windows
  MINKEY=<keys>       minimise the active window (default ctrl alt m)
  MINALLKEY=<keys>    minimise all (default ctrl alt shift m)
  AHKEY=<keys>        auto-hide on/off (default ctrl alt h)
  SHELLASK            Shell icons ask first ("Execute a file")
  SHELLDROP=<n>       1 = icons dropped onto a Shell typed (default), 0 = off
  UNPACKTO=<n>        archives unpack: 0 = Shell's drawer, 1 = RAM:,
                      2 = UNPACKDIR
  UNPACKDIR=<drawer>  the drawer for UNPACKTO=2
                      (none: the key is off)

Settings are applied in this order: icon tooltypes, settings file, then
Shell command line. Each overrides the preceding source.

To run multiple docks, give a settings file a project icon and set
SimpleDock as its default tool. Double-click it to start another dock
using that settings file.


AREXX
-----

SimpleDock's ARexx port is SIMPLEDOCK (a second dock uses the name of its
settings file). Every command returns a code: 0 = success,
5 = busy (you are dragging a dock icon - try again), 10 = failed (no such
icon, file or setting, or a value that is not valid). Commands marked
with -> also give a RESULT (use OPTIONS RESULTS in your script).

Identify an icon by its label, file name or path (case-insensitive),
or by its position: #1 is the first icon.

The dock

  SHOW                    open the dock again after HIDE
  HIDE                    close the dock window; SimpleDock keeps running
  REVEAL                  the same as pressing the hotkey
  REFRESH                 reopen the dock (a fresh look at the screen)
  GETPOS               -> "x y w h" of the dock, then "x y w h" of the
                          icon clicked last (if any)
  QUIT                    quit SimpleDock

Icons

  LIST                 -> the icons' paths, one per line, in dock order
  LIST NAMES           -> the icons' labels, one per line
  ADD <path> [AT <n> | AFTER <icon> | BEFORE <icon>] [LABEL <text>]
                          add an icon (at the end unless a place is
                          given); for a disk give its name, e.g. Work:
  REMOVE <icon>           remove an icon from the dock (not from disk)
  MOVE <icon> AT <n> | AFTER <icon> | BEFORE <icon>
                          move an icon

Settings

  SET <setting> ...       change one or more settings using the same
                          keywords as the settings file and tooltypes;
                          parentheses disable a setting; enclose values
                          containing spaces in quotes
  GET [<setting>]      -> the value of one setting: its value, 1 / 0 for
                          a switch that is on / off, empty when it is not
                          set (the default); without a setting: all
                          settings, one per line
  SAVE [<file>]           save settings and icons (to the dock's own
                          settings file unless a file is given)
  RELOAD                  read the dock's own settings file again
                          (unsaved changes are dropped)
  LOAD <file>             read another settings file into the dock
  SETTINGS [ADVANCED]     open the settings window (folded out)
  BENCH                -> drawing times on your machine (the dock
                          flickers while it runs)

ARexx changes are saved when you use SAVE or make a manual change that
saves the configuration. Settings supplied on the Shell command line
at startup remain in force. SET closes an open settings window and
keeps any changes made there.

Examples

  /* show the dock, open the settings */
  address SIMPLEDOCK 'SHOW'
  address SIMPLEDOCK 'SETTINGS'

  /* add Ram Disk after the Shell, then save */
  address SIMPLEDOCK 'ADD "RAM:" AFTER Shell LABEL "Ram Disk"'
  address SIMPLEDOCK 'SAVE'

  /* auto-hide on and the corners square, then read a value back */
  options results
  address SIMPLEDOCK 'SET AUTOHIDE=3 RADIUS=0'
  address SIMPLEDOCK 'GET AUTOHIDE'
  say 'Auto-hide speed:' result

  /* list the icons */
  options results
  address SIMPLEDOCK 'LIST NAMES'
  say result

  /* a different hotkey */
  address SIMPLEDOCK 'SET HOTKEY="ctrl alt x"'


LIMITATIONS
-----------

Running programs

  - AmigaOS keeps no list of running programs, so SimpleDock knows them
    by their windows and their names. Programs that have no window,
    rename their task or run from a script are not seen; a click then
    starts a new copy.
  - Standard console (Shell) windows have no IDCMP port, so they
    cannot be found like other windows. Shells are counted from
    AmigaDOS's list of Shells, and each Shell's console is asked for
    its window. A console that doesn't answer (some replacements) gives
    a count but no windows in the list. A Shell still running its
    startup script is counted once it is done.
  - A program that SimpleDock did not start is noticed when one of its
    windows becomes active, or within 2 seconds while the dock is on
    screen.
  - Minimise needs AmigaOS 3.2 (Intuition's HideWindow), or at least
    layers.library 45 (as in 3.1.4 and 3.9; the dock then hides the
    window's layers itself, and a program that brings its own window
    forward may show it again). Minimised windows show a pulsing dot
    under the icon; quitting SimpleDock shows them all again.
  - Close, Close All and Del send the message of the window's close
    gadget (a Shell gets Ctrl+\). The program may ask first, for
    example to save its work. A window without a close gadget cannot
    be closed this way (beep). A program that has not answered yet is
    not asked again until it does (beep).
  - A program that is not on the dock and whose file is not found gets
    the standard icon, and Shift+click on it only beeps. To keep it on
    the dock, a file requester asks where it is.
  - If you quit SimpleDock while programs it started are still running,
    they keep running; a few hundred bytes for each stay in memory until
    you reboot.

The dock

  - The dock is always one row of icons.
  - AmigaOS 3.2 windows cannot be round or transparent, so the rounded
    corners and the Transparent look use a picture of what is behind the
    dock. When something behind it changes, the dock steps aside for a
    moment to take a new picture (a short blink).
  - With auto-hide, icons cannot be dropped onto the hidden dock:
    Workbench locks the screen during an icon drag, so the dock cannot
    appear. Press the hotkey before starting the drag.
  - Over a Shell window, a hover label can leave a faint box behind
    (Shell windows redraw only part of what a closed window uncovers).
  - In front gives up after bringing the dock forward three times in a
    row with no key or button used in between (another window that
    keeps itself in front would otherwise fight it forever), and does
    nothing while the settings window is open.
  - On a full dock with programs running, the last place lists them
    all, the Workbench icon included.
  - A new screen mode for Workbench (for example from Prefs/ScreenMode)
    needs all windows closed, the dock's too: Intuition then asks to
    close a window it calls "<No title>" (the dock has no title bar).
    Quit SimpleDock first (or send it ARexx HIDE; Show / Hide Dock only
    slides it away) and start it again afterwards (or press the hotkey).

Older systems (before 3.2)

  - Without ReAction's chooser.gadget (3.1.4) the lists of the settings
    window are cycle gadgets, which step to the next choice on a click.
  - A double-click marking all of a text field needs 3.2.
  - Minimise needs 3.2, or layers.library 45 (3.1.4, 3.9), see above.
  - On 3.5 Workbench has no menus of their own for programs: Settings,
    About, Show/Hide and Quit are in the Tools menu ("SimpleDock
    Settings...").

Settings

  - A dock made from a drawer of icons (CONFIG set to a drawer) keeps
    its settings in its icon's tooltypes, and saves only the basic
    settings there. Advanced settings changed in the settings window,
    such as Running programs, are not kept; add them to the tooltypes
    by hand.
  - Version 1.0 does not know the settings new in 1.1 and shows such
    lines of the settings file as icons marked "?". Remove those icons,
    or the lines, if you go back to 1.0.
  - On a screen 640 pixels wide the Advanced side is wider than the
    screen and is cut at the right edge; a wider screen mode, or the
    settings file, gets to those settings.


BUG REPORTS
-----------

Report problems through the Issues page at:
https://github.com/renewable-intel/SimpleDock/issues

Describe the steps that led to the problem and what happened. Include
your CPU, graphics card or AGA chipset, and screen mode.

If SimpleDock crashes, enable Crash reports in the advanced settings,
then repeat the steps that caused the crash. If it crashes again,
SimpleDock writes ENVARC:SimpleDock-crash.txt, displays a message and
closes. The report includes the error, its location in the program, and
your system and settings. Attach the file to your issue.

Crash reporting adds no overhead during normal operation. It only
catches errors within SimpleDock; reboot if the whole system becomes
unstable.


HISTORY
-------

1.1 (16.10.2026)
  - Running programs: a dot under the icon, and the number of copies on
    it. A click brings all of a running program's windows to the front
    (again: one after another), Shift+click starts a new copy.
  - Long press on a running program: a list of its windows, to bring
    one to the front or close it (Del, Close, Close All). Slide along
    the dock to drag the icon instead.
  - Del over a running program's icon closes it.
  - SimpleDock starts programs itself, so it knows at once when they end.
  - The Shell icon counts open Shells and works like a program icon.
  - Programs not on the dock show at its end, behind a divider; drag one
    in front of it to keep it, or off the dock to hide it. A full dock
    lists the rest.
  - Files dropped onto a drawer or disk icon are copied into it.
  - Workbench icon for the open drawer windows; docked drawers and disks
    that are open get a dot and their windows' list.
  - Minimise..., Show... and Close... pick one window at a time.
  - New Advanced settings (under Programs): Running programs, Click when
    running, Start programs with, Drop on a drawer, Long press, Programs
    not on the dock, Workbench icon, Hidden programs (a list with
    Remove).
  - Settings window in groups with headings (Place, Icons, Look;
    Fine-tune, Programs, System); Opacity greyed out while Transparent
    is Off; Recall is now "Undo changes".
  - Dock menu: Show / Hide Dock.
  - Settings window: a double-click marks all of a text field.
  - Auto-hide: the dock stays out a little beyond its ends.
  - Bigger "?" on missing icons when the icons are large.
  - The dock is 3 pixels taller (room for the dot).

1.0 (15.10.2026)  First release.


